what's up guys today we're going to be  making tatsu aka  chinese barbecue pork it's sweet it's  savory  and super popular cantonese dish tattoo  is iconic for its red color it's  actually like  bright red and for those of you guys  that aren't used to seeing  tattoo it might be kind of like was that  raw as a blood  it's not it's just food coloring so if  you've ever seen  at chinese barbecue shops that hang  meats and it's like super red  that's tatsu the color red and chinese  culture represents good luck and good  fortune so i'm  pretty sure there's some sort of  auspicious reasoning there so in  cantonese  tahsu literally translates to fork roast  tah meaning fork siu meaning roast so  the traditional way of cooking tatsu is  skewering  a piece of meat with a fork and roasting  over fires  hence fork roast now i usually oven  roast mine but today we're going to try  and replicate that open fire cooking  style by using my traeger to barbecue  some cha  soup so let's get started  all right first up ingredients we're  gonna need three pounds of pork shoulder  aka pork butt whatever you like to call  it we'll need two thirds cup of sugar  six tablespoons of soy sauce three  tablespoons of rice wine one tablespoon  of oyster sauce  one tablespoon of hoisin sauce one  tablespoon of sesame oil one tablespoon  of ginger powder  one tablespoon of garlic powder 1.5  teaspoon five spice  one teaspoon of white pepper and one  teaspoon of salt  [Music]  all right let's get started so first  we're going to trim the fat of the pork  shoulder so mine has a fat cap so i'm  going to trim that in any excess fat  that i don't think is going to render  out  in the cook process but be careful don't  trim too much because if you trim too  much you won't have enough fat to  keep your meat moist and juicy so the  idea here is to have a thin layer of fat  some meat fat meat fat meat fat and not  a whole chunk of fat and if you see a  huge chunk of fat  feel free to cut that out once you've  trimmed out the fat let's go ahead and  cut up  our pork into thin slices about two  inches thick  and once you're done with that place  these two inch strips of pork into a  dish  all right next up super easy is the  marinade  literally add all the ingredients  together into a bowl  and mix so go ahead and combine your  sugar soy sauce  rice wine oyster sauce hoisin sauce  sesame oil  and all the dry spices and once you have  it all mixed together  let's add the red food coloring now i  don't usually use red food coloring when  i make it at home but for you guys and  for this video i wanted to make it look  extra cool so we're gonna add that red  food coloring today now it doesn't  actually affect the taste it's really  just for the cool looks  and if you want to bring in some good  fortune be my guest so  add one quarter teaspoon of red food  coloring and mix that in as well now  once you have the marinade ready  pour the marinade over your pork and i  like to work in the marinade into the  meat and into all the crevices by  massaging the meat  and the marinade together and after that  let it sit and marinate for at least  four hours and if you can afford it  one whole night would be ideal  [Music]  all right so let's fire up the trigger  now and bring it up to temp today i'll  be cooking with applewood pellets  and we'll be roasting at a temperature  of 350 degrees  once the trigger is up to temp go ahead  and toss in our meat onto the grill  and let the trigger do its magical work  and we're going to be roasting this for  15 minutes so after 15 minutes of  grilling we're going to baste our meat  with the leftover marinade  we want to reinforce the flavors and we  want to add some moisture  so it doesn't dry out during the cook  then flip and baste the other side as  well  and now we're going to let it roast for  another 10 minutes now while the meat is  roasting for these 10 minutes let's go  ahead and make our finishing glaze  start with a tablespoon of hot water add  in 1 tablespoon of brown sugar 2  tablespoons of honey and 1 tablespoon of  the leftover marinade and mix  [Music]  after those 10 minutes of roasting we  are ready to apply our honey glaze  you want to coat the meat with as much  of this as you can on both sides this  place is going to add a nice sweet outer  layer to your chatsu and once you've  applied the glaze go ahead and crank up  the temperature on the trigger too high  and now on my settings it literally says  high there's no temperature that goes  like no number temperature that goes  with it my trigger on high  reaches about 435 degrees i think  ideally trying to reach 450 but maybe  weather conditions don't allow it to go  to 450. so if you're at home if you're  doing this in the oven  try 435 degrees and we're going to cook  it for another 10  minutes at high temp the high heat will  char the sugar content  in the glaze and leave us with a  beautiful deep dark red pigment  on the tattoo and that's what we want  and after 10 minutes of high heat turn  off the traeger  and let it sit for another 10 minutes in  residual heat  [Music]  then pop it off the grill let's slice up  our beautiful fork  roast now what we're looking for as  we're cutting it is to cut it into  bite-sized pieces  just like most chinese food when you eat  with chopstick stanley style everything  is bite-sized  personally i like to angle the meat a  little bit so i can cut diagonally so i  can get longer slender pieces  this way it actually benefits the  texture of the bite as well  tossing can be eaten with rice noodles  bread boughs  any sort of starch or you can just serve  it up like cold cuts just like this  but my favorite way of eating tattoo is  to serve it with the better rice  add a little bit of greens like bok choy  or choy sum and then fry an  egg and place it on top of the whole  thing drizzle a little bit of soy sauce  on top and then you have one of the most  nostalgic meals of my childhood  cheers  i don't know what it is about cooking on  the fire  in the trigger but it almost accentuates  the marinade way more than the oven does  the oven kind of leaves it a little bit  more bland  but maybe it's the smell cooks into that  sugary glaze  and it's beautiful gotta get another  bite  so good like always like and subscribe  and if you decide to give this recipe a  try  don't forget to tag me in your creations  i'd love to check it out till next time  [Music]
 

